# Per-release checksums: generated at release time and uploaded as a release
# asset, which is where users verify against. Keeping copies in the repo just
# accumulates stale duplicates of what GitHub already serves.
SHA256SUMS-*.txt

# Documentation intermediate build artifacts
release_docs_*/*.json
*.prep.json
*.filled.json
checksums.sha256

# Internal Automation & Build Scripts - NEVER UPLOAD TO GITHUB
/scripts/
/Documentation.Scripts/
*.py
# GORILLA: exception — the vendored pfind engine is SOURCE, not a local script.
# It is embedded into the binary (internal/llm/tools/find.go) and shipped in
# the .deb; it must be in the repo on every machine that builds it.
!internal/llm/tools/pfind.py
*.sh

# Third-party leaked prompts kept LOCAL for study, not redistributed
system-prompts/reference/

# Release checksums are build outputs, regenerated per release. One got
# committed by the release pipeline's `git add -A` because nothing ignored it.
*.sha256
checksums.txt

# Session handoff / working state — deliberately local.
# Describes in-flight work and past mistakes in detail; useful to the next
# session on this machine, nobody else's business.
HANDOFF.md

# backup binaries (local safety copies)
gorilla-opencode.backup
*.backup

# Scratch/working folder — deliberately NOT in the repo.
#
# It was purged from all history on 2026-08-05 (it had been tracked since before
# the ignore files inside it existed, and a .gitignore cannot untrack something
# already tracked). The rules INSIDE that folder only cover its contents, so the
# directory itself would reappear the moment a new file landed there and the
# release pipeline ran `git add -A`. Named here so it cannot come back.
TO.DO.TO.FIX/

# GORILLA: built packages (see scripts/build-deb.sh)
Compiled.Builds/

# GORILLA: the project's own memory — NONE of it is published.
#
# Corrected 2026-08-14 by the owner. This rule first covered only
# LESSONS/chroma_opencode/ (the binary vector store), on the reasoning that the
# XML atoms were "tracked knowledge" and should be committed. That was wrong and
# the original line is replaced rather than kept, because two contradictory
# rules in a .gitignore is how something gets published by accident.
#
# LESSONS/ is written for US and for the models working here. The atoms name
# real failures, real hours lost, internal paths and how this machine is set up.
# That is working material, not documentation for strangers, and it goes in the
# same category as /scripts/ and /Documentation.Scripts/ above — deliberately
# untracked by an earlier security decision.
#
# The whole directory, not a file list: the release pipeline runs `git add -A`,
# so anything added under LESSONS/ later would be swept in silently. Same reason
# TO.DO.TO.FIX/ is named above.
#
# The store is rebuildable from the atoms at any time:
#   SECOND.BRAIN/vector_env/bin/python3 LESSONS/ingest_lessons.py
LESSONS/

# GORILLA OVERRIDE: local output of the code-review toolkit
# (Documentation.Scripts/Code.review/), one timestamped directory per run.
# Untracked but not ignored, it made `git status` dirty, and Go stamps that
# into every binary as vcs.modified=true — so release builds claimed to come
# from a modified tree when the tracked source was clean. The directory itself
# is left in place; only its tracking is suppressed.
.code_review/
# dual_track.py working files — local only, never published
PAYLOAD.*
PRECHECK.json
